# SymbiYosys job for the AER event FIFO.
#
#   sby -f aer_fifo.sby prove      k-induction proof at default parameters
#                                  (WIDTH=16, DEPTH=64, docs/10 EVQ sizing)
#   sby -f aer_fifo.sby prove_d4   same proof at DEPTH=4, WIDTH=8 (fast
#                                  parameterization check)
#   sby -f aer_fifo.sby bmc        bounded check, depth 40
#   sby -f aer_fifo.sby cover      reachability of full/drain/drop states,
#                                  P6, which pins the read port to the
#                                  registered (not show-ahead) shape that
#                                  two consumers depend on, P9, a
#                                  corrected pointer upset during traffic,
#                                  and P13, a detected entry upset during
#                                  traffic followed by a good delivery
#
# The properties live in formal/aer_fifo_props.v, textually included at
# the end of the aer_fifo module body under `ifdef FORMAL (read_verilog
# -formal defines FORMAL; both files land in the same sby src directory,
# so the include resolves).
#
# Every task above runs with pointer fault injection enabled, and there
# is deliberately no task without it. hw/rtl/aer_fifo.v triplicates and
# votes both queue pointers (docs/16 section 5.2), and under `ifdef
# FORMAL it declares six free vectors that corrupt one replica each on
# the way to the voter, constrained only to at most one faulty replica
# per pointer. The fault-free design is the case where the solver picks
# zero, so P1..P6 are proven in the presence of the fault rather than in
# its absence, and P7/P8 add the masking and the exact reporting. A
# separate fault-free task would prove a strictly weaker statement about
# a design that does not exist, and a separate faulty task would let the
# two drift apart.
#
# The same is true of the ENTRY-PARITY fault (docs/29): a seventh free
# vector corrupts the fetched codeword, constrained to the weight one
# check bit can claim, and P10..P12 say the corrupted entry is discarded
# and reported rather than delivered. It is on in all four tasks for the
# same reason, and P5's readout assertions are qualified by rd_pass
# rather than rd_ok so that they state what the hardened design does.
#
# Cost of that decision, measured on the pinned toolchain (yosys 0.67+146,
# yices) so that a future slowdown has a reference, and because one of
# these numbers is large enough that it has to be defended rather than
# discovered [all fact, 2026-08-29]:
#
#   prove     41 s      bmc  5 min 48 s      prove_d4  1 s
#   cover     29 min 19 s, against 3 min 57 s for the same job on the
#             pre-TMR sources
#
# Re-measured 2026-08-30 with the entry-parity fault of docs/29 added,
# same toolchain, otherwise idle machine [fact]:
#
#   prove     80 s      bmc  12 min 11 s     prove_d4  3.58 s
#   cover     41 min 55 s
#
# Roughly 2x on the three assertion-carrying jobs, and it is the fault
# model rather than the properties: seventeen more free bits per cycle
# over the fetched codeword. The properties themselves are cheap in the
# form that shipped and were expensive in the form that did not -- an
# earlier P10 stated over all DEPTH slots took prove past ten minutes
# without converging, which formal/aer_fifo_props.v records next to the
# code that replaced it.
#
# The cover job is the whole cost, and it is the fault model rather than
# the redundancy that buys it. Measured on the same machine, same job,
# three fault models [fact]:
#
#   injection vectors tied to zero (TMR present, no fault)   6 min 24 s
#   pattern per replica `anyconst`, applied freely in time  24 min 51 s
#   pattern free every cycle (what is implemented)          29 min 19 s
#
# So the redundancy itself costs about two and a half minutes and the
# freedom of the fault costs the rest. The middle row is what makes the
# choice easy: weakening the fault model to a fixed-per-trace pattern
# gives back four and a half minutes of the twenty-three, which is not
# worth a weaker theorem in the module whose whole purpose is tolerating
# that fault. It also buys nothing back on the three jobs that carry
# P1..P8: bmc is faster than the pre-TMR run and prove is 41 s.
#
# Two levers exist if this ever has to come down. (1) Drop injection from
# the cover job alone, which also drops P9, the anti-vacuity check on the
# fault model -- read the P8/P9 note in aer_fifo_props.v before doing
# that. (2) The `btor btormc` engine returns pass on this job in under a
# second, which is 1700x, and that is exactly why it is not used here
# without someone first confirming that the deep cover
# (`empty && f_seen_full`, reached by yices at step 130) is genuinely
# reached and not silently skipped. A 0.7-second pass where the pinned
# engine needs 29 minutes is a result to verify, not to adopt.
#
# The file list is aer_fifo.v and the property file, and it has to stay
# that way: aer_fifo carries its own pointer-bank module and its own
# inline majority rather than instantiating hw/rtl/tmr_voter.v, precisely
# so that this job and hw/tb/Makefile can elaborate the queue from one
# file. See the "pointer TMR" section of hw/rtl/aer_fifo.v.

[tasks]
bmc
prove
prove_d4
cover

[options]
bmc: mode bmc
bmc: depth 40
prove: mode prove
prove: depth 15
prove_d4: mode prove
prove_d4: depth 15
cover: mode cover
cover: depth 150

[engines]
smtbmc yices

[script]
read_verilog -formal aer_fifo.v
prove_d4: chparam -set DEPTH 4 -set WIDTH 8 aer_fifo
prep -top aer_fifo

[files]
../hw/rtl/aer_fifo.v
aer_fifo_props.v
